#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128986 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129167 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64d342be-ebaf-5e94-b641-ea8ce6f14c12" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Type I Regions"}] :question/id #uuid "9cbb7321-753d-5253-8b64-072e3cc227bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6101" :question/problem "![](images/76/766a7abf73e9f9ef85164b1165166d7493cd36cdc75d0f244e475fed8aeea8fd.png)\n\nRepresent the type I region above using set notation." :question/worked-solution "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: a \\leq x  \\leq b, \\quad g(x) \\leq y \\leq f(x) \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type I region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $x.$\n\n- The curve $x=y-1$ can be written as $y=x+1.$\n\n- The curve $y=7-x$ is already written as a function of $x.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=3.$ So, we have $1 \\leq x \\leq 3.$\n\nWe draw a vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower function $g(x)$ and the upper function $f(x)$. A vertical line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type I region.\n\n![](images/ac/acf870e74a9029543a6e6b21995c293f67f251b54dfad9584220780bc3a0b454.png)\n\nThe vertical line enters the region through the lower function $y=x+1$ and leaves through the upper function $y=7-x.$ So, we have $x+1 \\leq y \\leq 7-x.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD= \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\: x+1 \\leq y \\leq 7-x \\big\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128987 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129168 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85335de6-e1dc-5a2f-b30a-806627759030" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Type II Regions"}] :question/id #uuid "cce0a7b2-7b25-5da6-8aa2-cbe8a40cd522" :question/math-academy-id "e-6102" :question/problem "![](images/39/3919a6a8442e983a9ca3465b2ed9abbeda18b7dc8d21b7c74b1851b53c1be714.png)\n\nRepresent the type II region above using set notation." :question/worked-solution "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: a \\leq y  \\leq b, \\quad g(y) \\leq x \\leq f(y) \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type II region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $y.$\n\n- The curve $y = 2-x^2$ can be written as $x = -\\sqrt{2-y}.$ Note that we have picked the negative square root since $x\\leq 0$ in our region.\n\n- The curve $x=\\dfrac{y-2}{2}$ is already written as a function of $y.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the horizontal lines $y=-2$ and $y=2.$ So, we have $-2 \\leq y \\leq 2.$\n\nWe draw a horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left function $g(y)$ and the right function $f(y)$. A horizontal line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type II region.\n\n![](images/0c/0cf258d0269db0977d5c19fe7abd159f13319bb493394fbd049e5660d88a1d6f.png)\n\nThe horizontal line enters the region through the lower function $x=-\\sqrt{2-y}$ and leaves through the upper function $x=\\dfrac{y-2}{2}.$ So, we have $-\\sqrt{2-y} \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{y-2}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD= \\bigg\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\: -\\sqrt{2-y} \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{y-2}{2} \\bigg\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128988 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither"}] :question/id #uuid "cd9ce5bf-1663-5110-8aaa-8f0f47d7d30a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6103" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the parabola $y=x^2$ and the line $y=4?$\n\n- It is a type I region\n\n- It is a type II region\n\n- It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\ny=x^2\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx = \\pm\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/59/59d32c44bae848d52adc188e15a8bab470b3eccd887cad45c231b66c486284fc.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: x^2 \\leq y \\leq 4 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 4, \\: -\\sqrt{y} \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f978a9-f4d6-5a10-9c8b-281c5b614098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281767} {:answer/id #uuid "a2974d43-0f4f-5d74-81b4-509779cb407d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283107} {:answer/id #uuid "afc05a81-8007-5b6e-a964-8e1c3498a58f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283209} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a27724-0ceb-51d4-a944-01e3110d2e3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283219} {:answer/id #uuid "f6755bc6-84e9-55dd-8499-a10c6d535542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afc05a81-8007-5b6e-a964-8e1c3498a58f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283209} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7f688da-c900-5731-9711-90f24f686e61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0adcc01-b22f-5ba4-864b-30820cc15f4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-168841" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the triangle bounded between the lines $y=1-x,$ $y=x-1,$ and $x=0?$\n\n- It is a type I region\n- It is a type II region\n- It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=1-x\\, & ⟹\\,x=1-y \\\\ y=x-1\\, & ⟹\\,x=y+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/27/27503f983e3e02fa748bb8ef32966a6cd3b5fff29082ffaf3c10d0521b630e93.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x-1\\le y\\le 1-x}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le 1-|y|}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50c91312-6d25-5d77-a03c-b9737dcd0907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401588} {:answer/id #uuid "3070b0e6-2443-59e9-88ad-5d64b7dbe0c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401589} {:answer/id #uuid "de9e4a77-4bdb-50ab-8c39-a254aabe63cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401590} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed4ec19-9461-5c93-8e53-e713230511d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401591} {:answer/id #uuid "6572c207-612f-5e17-bdb8-cc85be4bf3b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de9e4a77-4bdb-50ab-8c39-a254aabe63cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401590} :answer-field/id #uuid "e37c6cf2-31b1-549d-9c9e-1baf99bb11bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fd6f7437-bc45-50cb-9400-bcd422c5b408" :question/math-academy-id "q-168921" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=1?$\n\nI. It is a type I region\nII. It is a type II region\nIII. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/bc/bc6b71d1fe69abd34dbd939dc62ede5201b75713e25456cf084e889bc44f449e.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The vertical line $x=0.4$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type I region.\n\n![](images/4a/4a1fd7069abaa3b3fa4d9c286773d226798bf3ad565e85663cab44557fd635c1.png)\n\n- The horizontal line $y=-1$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](images/26/260e8e959ca95a029a98bd4102106a66df418ea73c0640398c8fdebfdbf812f6.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129167 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64d342be-ebaf-5e94-b641-ea8ce6f14c12" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Type I Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c21d20b-aee6-537b-945a-9e284af99eca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337301} {:answer/id #uuid "9299674f-fe79-50f1-be3a-680d0ad1bd07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3e/3edfe230c7156c19aedb2abe318717048bc22fba9475dadccc41047fcc0a6b53.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337368} {:answer/id #uuid "a41cbdff-c661-5ef4-89bf-4764e15ce5cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8e/8e3cc81bc5269e4ae85516f2173e1d61b47a1c887f9e1532f914226333e09184.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337523} {:answer/id #uuid "aee6a291-804a-5e2b-b61e-0916ac387d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/82/82ed2a77b4017b904ea94ed11e7febb76385bc64082acee5ad3c2e1304fae22c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337601} {:answer/id #uuid "b62f4ce0-a3db-5bb1-96a4-de985fa7f700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/84/847a7a418ef19d023ba16122c7c0da3cf400a5467001c51ee0315b5c978a17db.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9299674f-fe79-50f1-be3a-680d0ad1bd07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3e/3edfe230c7156c19aedb2abe318717048bc22fba9475dadccc41047fcc0a6b53.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337368} :answer-field/id #uuid "72dd7ff5-253d-5388-9adb-ec7d9c2c5034" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc996932-2b25-55a2-8867-4e8b12518ba1" :question/math-academy-id "q-71809" :question/problem "Which of the following is a type I region in two-dimensional space?" :question/worked-solution "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,g(x)\\le y\\le f(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nA type I region has the property that a vertical line drawn through the region intersects the region in a single interval only.\n\nAmong the given options, the only type I region is the following.\n\n![](images/d7/d74059ee4370e106b54917ec43b007327805049033752867473d08da850001bf.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129838 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129167 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64d342be-ebaf-5e94-b641-ea8ce6f14c12" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Type I Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00940f70-313c-5053-b7f5-8cda00cea8b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y-3},\\,1\\le y\\le 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336034} {:answer/id #uuid "80cec75a-f5d0-5957-8bad-7e3a4224991a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y-3},\\,1\\le y\\le 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337200} {:answer/id #uuid "a11a9c7d-34d6-5d34-8d0d-c8270f2699be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337489} {:answer/id #uuid "cd031f1c-0aeb-5eab-9cfc-90a6a496a180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337829} {:answer/id #uuid "ee48561b-4f55-5654-8455-42c6cdce1f03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le {x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd031f1c-0aeb-5eab-9cfc-90a6a496a180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337829} :answer-field/id #uuid "cac360d2-57d9-57d3-b6c0-afa920e3365f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f6ac07a-f53a-5686-9107-a7ecc32ad390" :question/math-academy-id "q-71816" :question/problem "![](images/43/438b119b6fd2c2f066705f9f44053faa766e3f57b6ee1a614794febf7a517a12.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the shaded region above as a type I region in two-dimensional space?" :question/worked-solution "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,g(x)\\le y\\le f(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type I region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $x:$\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{y-3}\\,⟹\\,y={x}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nThe function $y=1$ is already written as a function of $x.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ So, we have $0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\nWe draw a vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower function $g(x)$ and the upper function $f(x).$ A vertical line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type I region.\n\n![](images/43/43b35101f6c57352975f3667ca76dece5782326c9af008990c8820a71bed504d.png)\n\nThe vertical line enters the region through the lower function $y=1$ and leaves through the upper function $y={x}^{2}+3.$ So, we have $1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+3.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129839 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129168 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85335de6-e1dc-5a2f-b30a-806627759030" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Type II Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb1638ba-1d0a-5da1-b7e4-2f08f45f829e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e3/e3e87ba472c52ffd7f6df89f4ad9886e25a3d64cd076d90a085adb38ce6c1508.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128516} {:answer/id #uuid "310b1f99-2ddc-5ff7-abaa-c6dcc52e7c0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/28/28302f0f3d075a9a0b698613b479cf777c00ece0be6766de79c5ced5e01ff63d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128517} {:answer/id #uuid "31a38edb-3150-59ed-b9a2-5ca4530919a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128518} {:answer/id #uuid "38a2fee1-773a-5688-8132-96509ebe923c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7c/7c8b602894c58ee03f2e0cc3be9155d177da9915f537482671609de075a6bc80.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128519} {:answer/id #uuid "40974dcc-78f6-5b35-bca7-a00d9c372e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/73/73fce52068582bba934a9db9b3ccd130b7022a752eb3e753aa7b1